      "actual": "Write the declared common-diagonal Wick interaction as :W4:_C=W4+(1/2)q^T deltaK_C q+6C^2(g+4lambda), with deltaK_C=-3C[(g+lambda)I+lambda L_Q3]. Add a fixed renormalized quadratic matrix K_R=m_R I+eta_R L_Q3. Its raw quadratic Walsh eigenvalues are kappa_s=m_R+2s eta_R-3C(g+lambda+2s lambda), s=0,1,2,3. If b_C=(1/2)max(0,-min_s kappa_s) and epsilon_C=16b_C^2/g-6C^2(g+4lambda), then V_C=(1/2)q^T K_R q+:W4:_C+epsilon_C is at least g|q|^4/64. Thus, after this declared cutoff-dependent scalar shift, the enlarged finite-regulator Hamiltonians have a cutoff-uniform lower form bound in the Riemann-sum convention and retain the standard fixed-regulator ground/Gibbs construction. For any chosen states on the nested dyadic type-I algebras, compactness of the product of state spaces gives a subnet whose every fixed-regulator restriction converges to a compatible state on the inductive C-star limit. Conjugating the natural bulk embeddings by the registered unitary fixed-regulator cut anchors constructs exact matched-reference-cut monomorphisms, and the cut-state defect equals the bulk-state defect exactly. This kinematic square cannot be promoted to exact natural interacting dynamics equivariance within the declared inserted-one-dimensional g>0 family and quadratic-counterterm class: on the collective retained-zero/added-Nyquist plane, the fine force on P_X contains -(3g/L)X*Y^2, a cubic force term quadratic in the added mode, so the low algebra is not invariant. For ground projectors, define sigma_M=iota_(M,2M,*)(P_(2M)), eta_M=1-Tr(P_M sigma_M), and d_M=||sigma_M-P_M||_1; then 2eta_M<=d_M<=2sqrt(eta_M), while exact failure at each pair does not decide whether d_M tends to zero. Abstract compactness and lower stability do not imply regularity: squeezed one-oscillator Hamiltonians h_N=[N P^2+Q^2/N]/2 have a uniform spectrum and regular normal ground states but a nonregular pointwise Weyl limit. A positive continuum-state proof needs an additional regularity mechanism, such as uniform fixed-mode characteristic equicontinuity or moment bounds, and a full-sequence claim separately needs a Cauchy, uniqueness-plus-uniformity, or equivalent identification theorem.",
